        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was Assassin's Creed Valhalla released?\n\nAnswer A: Dec 6, 2022\n  about: Assassin's Creed Valhalla\n  sense: release date\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Released. Dec 6, 2022.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: November 1, 2021\n  about: Assassin's Creed Valhalla\n  sense: release date\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Valhalla was released for PlayStation 4, Windows, Xbox One, Xbox Series X and Series S, and Stadia on November 1, 2021, with the PlayStation 5 version\" [document 2]\n\nAnswer C: November 10, 2020\n  about: Assassin's Creed Valhalla\n  sense: release date\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Valhalla was released for PlayStation 4, Windows, Xbox One, Xbox Series X and Series S, and Stadia on November 10, 2020, with the PlayStation 5 version...\" [document 3]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Developer. Ubisoft Montreal ; Publisher. Ubisoft ; Released. Dec 6, 2022.\nDocument 2: Valhalla was released for PlayStation 4, Windows, Xbox One, Xbox Series X and Series S, and Stadia on November 1, 2021, with the PlayStation 5 version ...\nDocument 3: Valhalla was released for PlayStation 4, Windows, Xbox One, Xbox Series X and Series S, and Stadia on November 10, 2020, with the PlayStation 5 version ...\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. 